WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.86
This is journey twenty eight of Hewn, the software factory: configure automatic

00:00:06.86 --> 00:00:07.54
descoping.

00:00:07.54 --> 00:00:10.80
Stage nine estimates every design in ideal days.

00:00:10.80 --> 00:00:15.75
Anything above the descope threshold is split, or deferred with a reason.

00:00:15.75 --> 00:00:20.16
Until now that threshold was fixed in the server's configuration.

00:00:20.16 --> 00:00:23.49
This video shows an Owner setting it in Settings,

00:00:23.49 --> 00:00:25.86
and stage nine planning against it.

00:00:27.01 --> 00:00:32.76
In Settings, an Owner sets the descope threshold in ideal days, three by default.

00:00:32.76 --> 00:00:38.57
Stage nine uses it: a design above the threshold is first split into designs at or

00:00:38.57 --> 00:00:41.34
below it, and descoped if it cannot be.

00:00:41.34 --> 00:00:43.61
Viewers see the value read-only.

00:00:43.61 --> 00:00:47.79
This is for Owners, who decide how big an increment may be.

00:00:48.94 --> 00:00:53.97
Here an Owner opens Settings, under Direction defaults, beside the default budget.

00:00:53.97 --> 00:00:55.20
They try half a day.

00:00:55.20 --> 00:00:59.07
Save defaults refuses it: the error summary links to the field,

00:00:59.07 --> 00:01:04.16
and the field says the threshold must be a whole number of days from one to twenty.

00:01:04.16 --> 00:01:06.80
The link puts the cursor back in the field.

00:01:06.80 --> 00:01:08.03
One day is accepted.

00:01:08.03 --> 00:01:12.38
The callout says new directions start with a one-day descope threshold,

00:01:12.38 --> 00:01:15.33
and that directions already running keep theirs.

00:01:16.48 --> 00:01:22.13
The threshold is saved beside the default budget, in the same direction defaults row,

00:01:22.13 --> 00:01:24.59
through the same Owner-only endpoint.

00:01:24.59 --> 00:01:28.18
The validator takes a whole number from one to twenty,

00:01:28.18 --> 00:01:30.57
with the mock's message and summary.

00:01:30.57 --> 00:01:33.63
A save that leaves it out keeps the one saved.

00:01:33.98 --> 00:01:36.66
The callout's promise decides the design.

00:01:36.66 --> 00:01:40.85
A direction records the threshold in force when it is submitted,

00:01:40.85 --> 00:01:42.81
as it already does its budget.

00:01:42.81 --> 00:01:47.91
So changing the setting never changes the plan of a direction already running.

00:01:48.26 --> 00:01:50.47
This sequence shows the whole path.

00:01:50.47 --> 00:01:51.47
The Owner saves.

00:01:51.47 --> 00:01:55.06
Later a direction is submitted and records the threshold.

00:01:55.06 --> 00:01:59.53
When stage nine is leased, the lease carries the direction's threshold,

00:01:59.53 --> 00:02:03.43
or the current setting for directions from before this change.

00:02:04.58 --> 00:02:09.60
Stage nine's agent was told to split designs above the descope threshold,

00:02:09.60 --> 00:02:11.38
but never told the number.

00:02:11.38 --> 00:02:16.60
Now its task names it: split any design above the threshold of n ideal days.

00:02:16.60 --> 00:02:21.27
What is still above it after the agent's split, the descoper defers,

00:02:21.27 --> 00:02:22.37
with its reason.

00:02:22.72 --> 00:02:26.94
Here a new direction is submitted with the threshold at one day,

00:02:26.94 --> 00:02:28.33
and a worker runs it.

00:02:28.33 --> 00:02:32.68
The fake agent estimates every design at two days and splits none.

00:02:32.68 --> 00:02:38.08
When stage nine finishes, the Backlog shows each design deferred: estimated at two

00:02:38.08 --> 00:02:41.90
days, above the one-day threshold, and could not be split.

00:02:43.05 --> 00:02:45.74
The backend tests go through the real API.

00:02:45.74 --> 00:02:48.80
The threshold is three until an Owner saves one,

00:02:48.80 --> 00:02:51.55
and the next lease carries the saved value.

00:02:51.55 --> 00:02:54.62
Zero, twenty one and two and a half are refused.

00:02:54.62 --> 00:02:56.15
A Viewer gets forbidden.

00:02:56.15 --> 00:03:01.45
And a direction submitted before a change keeps the threshold it started with; that

00:03:01.45 --> 00:03:04.84
test failed first, with one where three was expected.

00:03:04.84 --> 00:03:07.71
All three hundred and six backend tests pass.

00:03:08.06 --> 00:03:12.31
The worker test checks the agent's task names the leased threshold.

00:03:12.31 --> 00:03:15.91
It failed first, then passed with the other worker tests.

00:03:16.26 --> 00:03:20.96
End to end, a behaviour test saves five days, and refuses half a day,

00:03:20.96 --> 00:03:24.90
twenty one and an empty field, each with its linked error.

00:03:24.90 --> 00:03:27.21
A Viewer sees the field read-only.

00:03:27.21 --> 00:03:32.18
Before the field existed, the first of these failed with nothing to find.

00:03:32.53 --> 00:03:36.92
A pipeline test sets one day in Settings, starts a real worker,

00:03:36.92 --> 00:03:42.42
and waits for stage nine to defer every two-day design with the one-day reason.

00:03:42.42 --> 00:03:48.27
Visual tests compare the field with its mocks, by default, invalid and for a Viewer.

00:03:49.42 --> 00:03:54.78
The Viewer sees the threshold read-only, with a notice that an Owner manages the

00:03:54.78 --> 00:03:55.38
defaults.

00:03:55.38 --> 00:03:59.00
The field reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:04:00.15 --> 00:04:02.36
Journey twenty eight is complete.

00:04:02.36 --> 00:04:05.37
Owners set the descope threshold in Settings,

00:04:05.37 --> 00:04:08.58
a whole number of ideal days from one to twenty.

00:04:08.58 --> 00:04:12.39
Each direction keeps the threshold it was submitted with.

00:04:12.39 --> 00:04:15.34
And stage nine splits and defers against it.

00:04:15.34 --> 00:04:17.41
Next comes journey twenty nine.
